* Westrobothnian: {{l|gmq-bot|haug}}
* {{desc|nrn|hjog}}, {{l|nrn|høg}}
* Norwegian Nynorsk: {{l|nn|håg}} (< pl. ''hávir''; obsolete, dialectal), {{l|nn|hå}} (in compound place names only)

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*hērą}}, from {{der|non|ine-pro|*keres-||rough hair, bristle}}. Compare Old Saxon and {{cog|goh|hār}}, {{cog|ang|her}}, {{m|ang|hǣr}}.

====Noun====
{{non-noun|n}}

# [[hair]]

=====Descendants=====
* Danish: {{l|da|hår}}
* Faroese: {{l|fo|hár}}
* Icelandic: {{l|is|hár}}
* Norwegian: {{l|no|hår}}
* Swedish: {{l|sv|hår}}

===Etymology 3===
{{rfe|non}}

====Noun====
{{non-noun|m|hás|háir}}

# [[spiny dogfish]]
#: {{Q|non|First Grammatical Treatise|84|22|quote=''Har'' vex á kykvendum, en '''''hȧr''''' er fiskr.|trans=''Hair'' grows on living things, but ''har'' is a fish.}}

=====Usage notes=====
The {{w|First Grammarian}} says that this word had a long nasalized vowel, marked with an overdot, and contrasts it with {{m|non|hár|t=hair|id=hair}}, which does not.

=====Descendants=====
* Faroese: {{l|fo|háur}}, {{l|fo|hávur}}
* Icelandic: {{l|is|hár}}

